From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winex11: a Linux program that sizes its own window keeps the width it chose

A Linux program's frame follows the program when it sizes its own window
(WM_X11DRV_SG_EMBED_SIZE, 0762). The message carries the new and the old
size packed as width | height << 16, and the frame took the whole packed
value as the width: every time a program sized itself, its frame was made
as wide as Wine allows a window -- wider than the screen -- and the
program's window was stretched to it. In the s14 regression walk
(2026-10-06, a 2736x1824 screen) LXTerminal and SG Office were 2750 px
wide after Settings went from 175% to 125% (GTK and Qt size their windows
for a new display scale); a dialog that fits itself to its contents does
the same. The width is now the low half alone (and the height the next).

Test: test/embedselfsize-gate.sh (an xterm sizing itself 600x300 and
420x380 keeps that width, its frame round it; mutant
SG_MUTANT_EMBED_SIZE_WIDTH fails it). embedscale, linuxembed, csdmaxloop,
embeddragmax and embedmaxgrab still pass.
---
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/sg_embed.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/sg_embed.c
@@ -1012,7 +1012,16 @@
     {
         /* the program sized its window: the frame follows */
         RECT rect;
+#ifndef SG_MUTANT_EMBED_SIZE_WIDTH
+        /* both are width | height << 16: the width is the low half alone --
+         * taken whole, a program that sized itself (GTK and Qt for a new
+         * display scale, a dialog fitting its contents) had its frame made
+         * as wide as Wine allows, wider than the screen (2750 px on a 2736
+         * px screen) */
+        int dw = (int)(wp & 0xffff) - (int)(lp & 0xffff), dh = (int)((wp >> 16) & 0xffff) - (int)((lp >> 16) & 0xffff);
+#else
         int dw = (int)wp - (int)(lp & 0xffff), dh = (int)(wp >> 16) - (int)(lp >> 16);
+#endif
 #ifndef SG_MUTANT_EMBED_SIZE_LOOP
         /* not while maximized or full screen: the frame's size is the
          * screen's then, as a window manager keeps it */
